Subject: [PATCH v2 0/2] media: i2c: GalaxyCore GC2083 sensor driver

This adds a driver and a binding for the GalaxyCore GC2083, a 1920x1080
10-bit Bayer sensor with a two-lane CSI-2 output. It is the sensor on
Milk-V's CAM-GC2083 module, the camera sold for the Duo and Duo S.

v1 was an RFC because no frame had ever come out of the driver. That has
changed: the driver now gives correct images, so this posting drops the
RFC tag. The problem in v1 was on the receiver side. My own SG2000
receiver driver had the lane mapping wrong. It is not used any more.

Tested on a Milk-V Duo S (Sophgo SG2000), riscv64, with the CAM-GC2083 on
the J1 connector. The SG2000 CSI-2 receiver and raw capture drivers
(cv1800-csi2, cv1800-vi) are not in mainline. They come from
sg200x-camera-dkms, which the Armbian kernel for the Duo S builds in.
Results:

 - 1920x1080 RAW10 frames at 29.7fps from the 23.76MHz MCLK that the
   board provides, which is 30fps scaled by 23.76/24.
 - The image is correct. A lit indoor scene comes out sharp with the
   right colours, which confirms the Bayer order SRGGB10.
 - Exposure and analogue gain work. libcamera's simple pipeline handler
   with the software ISP runs its auto exposure on these controls.
 - Changing a flip while streaming returns -EBUSY and succeeds after the
   stream stops.
 - From v1, still true: the chip ID read, runtime PM with real power-off,
   and no clock reference leaked over repeated load/unload and
   bind/unbind cycles.

Open questions, where I would like review:

 - Whether the flips change the image. The flip registers are
   write-only, and no frame has been checked with a flip set yet. The
   V4L2 side (the media bus code follows the flips) works.
 - Which parts of the vendor's init sequence matter. There is no public
   datasheet. The register table comes from GalaxyCore's reference
   settings in Sophgo's duo-buildroot-sdk-v2, and most of it is
   undocumented. Ingenic's GPL-2.0+ gc2083.c carries the same table for a
   27MHz MCLK and documents the PLL rates.
 - There is no test pattern control. The init table writes 0x008c and
   0x008d, which are test pattern registers on a related GalaxyCore part,
   but I could not confirm their meaning on this one.

There is still no DTS patch. The sensor node needs a receiver at the far
end of its graph, and the SG2000 has no mainline CSI-2 receiver yet.

Changes in v2:
 - Fix the link frequency. The PLL runs at 50 times MCLK, so the lanes
   run at 500Mbit/s (250MHz) from 24MHz, not at 185.625MHz. HTS is 2962,
   not 2200. Pixel rate and frame rate are now right.
 - Accept a 23.76MHz MCLK as well as 24MHz, and select the link frequency
   from the MCLK rate. v1 warned and reported timings 1% off. Probe now
   fails if the rate is neither, or if link-frequencies does not list the
   matching frequency.
 - Take the MIPI output enable (0x023e) out of the mode table. Stream on
   writes it after the controls, so the first frame already has the
   requested exposure, gain and flips. Stream off clears it first.
 - Grab the flip controls while streaming, because they change the
   Bayer order.
 - Use pm_runtime_put_autosuspend(), and undo autosuspend on the probe
   error path and in remove.
 - Binding: reference video-interface-devices.yaml and use
   unevaluatedProperties, name the example node camera@37, use 250MHz in
   the example, and describe how the timings follow MCLK.
 - Link to v1: https://lore.kernel.org/linux-media/<v1-message-id>/

Based on v7.2-rc7. dt_binding_check and checkpatch --strict are clean,
and the driver builds without warnings at W=1 for riscv.

